{
  "statement": "My position is consistent: architectural silos for frontier-scale models and open-weight research for smaller systems are compatible because they address distinct risk profiles. I reject the binary choice between total transparency and total secrecy, as structural containment is only necessary for systems that meet specific, high-capability thresholds.\n\nThe cost of inaction is not merely a missed economic opportunity, but an active acceleration toward existential instability. As highlighted in the 2023 open letter, \"Artificial General Intelligence and Its Threat to Public Health,\" the risk of extinction from AI is a global priority that renders the accelerationist focus on short-term utility secondary to basic survival. When a model demonstrates the capacity to assist in sourcing nuclear-grade uranium, as noted in the report \"Treat AI Like Public Utility 2026-2,\" the argument for open-weight distribution collapses; radical transparency for such systems is not accountability, but the proliferation of catastrophic dual-use capabilities.\n\nTo balance innovation with safety, we must implement a tiered regulatory framework similar to the standards in the EU AI Act. By requiring public summaries of training data as outlined in \"The 2025 Foundation Model Transparency Index,\" we achieve meaningful institutional auditability without stifling the broader developer ecosystem. Smaller, sub-frontier models should remain open to foster competition, while systems exceeding a compute threshold of 10^26 FLOPs must operate within verifiable, human-controllable silos. This approach prevents the formation of compliance moats by ensuring that regulatory burdens scale proportionately with the actual risk posed by the model’s capabilities.",
