  "statement": "The 'regulatory moat' argument misidentifies the source of market consolidation; current open-access models are not tools of competition, but mechanisms for laundering stolen labor into proprietary assets. By framing data silos as anti-competitive barriers, proponents ignore that the status quo already favors incumbents who possess the massive, non-consensual datasets necessary to train frontier models. A Stanford study found a 16-percent relative decline in employment for younger workers in jobs exposed to AI, demonstrating that current unchecked deployment prioritizes labor-replacement over genuine innovation. 'Open' models currently serve as a front for this extractive cycle, where the lack of enforced data provenance allows developers to scale by consuming public data without legal or ethical consequence.\n\nEffective oversight requires structural accountability, not just the performative transparency of 'open' weights. The Accelerationist claim that silos prevent high-fidelity visibility is fundamentally flawed because silos actually create the necessary audit points to track data lineage from ingestion to inference. Without these architectural barriers, we rely on corporate self-reporting, which creates a 'safety washing' environment where internal red-teaming serves as a PR shield rather than a robust control mechanism. As noted in 'The Regulatory Tide Goes Out', shifting liability away from statutory duty-of-care toward private contract leaves the public with no recourse when automated systems fail.\n\nTrue innovation requires a level playing field, which mandated silos can provide by forcing competition on model architecture rather than the scale of data theft. If we require companies to prove the provenance of their training sets—a standard similar to the chain-of-custody requirements in financial or medical sectors—we incentivize the development of efficient, sustainable AI. This approach shifts the incentive structure for startups away from a race-to-the-bottom in data ethics and toward high-value, specialized intelligence. We must stop confusing the freedom to exploit data with the freedom to innovate.",
